## Repository layout

```
pathways-analysis/
├── README.md
├── CLAUDE.md                     # working notes on the dataset's real semantics
├── pyproject.toml
├── .env.example
├── .gitignore
├── sql/                          # all analysis SQL, one file per concern
│   ├── 00_reference_data.sql     # dimension extracts + triage logic fact table
│   ├── 01_inventory.sql          # dataset inventory counts
│   ├── 02_pathway_complexity.sql # per-pathway structural metrics
│   ├── 03_question_reuse.sql     # node-reference reuse across pathways
│   ├── 04_answer_cardinality.sql # answer counts (Phase 2 CPT sizing input)
│   ├── 05_jump_analysis.sql      # JumpID profiling and classification
│   ├── 06_transfer_analysis.sql  # T1-T4 unpivot and target resolution
│   ├── 07_symptom_analysis.sql   # symptom groups and discriminators
│   ├── 08_snomed_analysis.sql    # SNOMED reach per pathway
│   ├── 09_data_quality.sql       # referential integrity and duplicate checks
│   ├── 09_family_features.sql    # PHASE 2: age/gender/trauma tags, disposition routes
│   ├── 10_family_overlap.sql     # PHASE 2: server-side native-grouping overlap
│   ├── 11_family_transitions.sql # PHASE 2: server-side edge list (cross-check)
│   └── 12_family_shared_features.sql  # PHASE 2: feature reuse across pathways
├── config/
│   └── family_discovery.yaml     # PHASE 2: every threshold, weight and method choice
├── src/pathways_analysis/
│   ├── config.py                 # env-var settings, path discovery
│   ├── db.py                     # read-only engine, query loader, safety guard
│   ├── graph.py                  # graph building, metrics, communities, similarity
│   ├── exports.py                # table/graph/chart writing and chart theme
│   ├── profiling.py              # Phase 1 runner and CLI
│   ├── family_config.py          # Phase 2 configuration loading and validation
│   ├── family_features.py        # per-pathway feature model, NHS-native candidates
│   ├── family_overlap.py         # overlap matrix, high-overlap evidence, merges
│   ├── family_evaluation.py      # cohesion, splits, scorecard, strategy comparison
│   ├── family_boundaries.py      # boundary pathways and cross-family features
│   ├── family_core.py            # shared-core candidate features
│   ├── family_routing.py         # pathway-to-family affinity and routing ambiguity
│   ├── family_charts.py          # Phase 2 figures
│   ├── family_report.py          # evidence report and clinical review pack
│   ├── family_discovery.py       # Phase 2 runner, hybrid strategies and CLI
│   └── report_builder.py         # REPORTING: analytical CSVs -> scoped presentation JSON
├── reports/                      # REPORTING: static HTML briefing (see below)
│   ├── index.html                # executive landing page
│   ├── phase1.html               # Phase 1 report
│   ├── phase2.html               # Phase 2 report
│   ├── phase3.html               # Phase 3 placeholder — "Not yet started"
│   ├── css/report.css
│   ├── js/{common,charts,phase1,phase2}.js
│   ├── vendor/                   # Plotly, vendored (MIT) — no external requests
│   └── data/                     # generated JSON, gitignored
├── tests/
├── data/processed/               # gitignored outputs
└── outputs/
    ├── tables/                   # gitignored CSVs (Phase 1)
    ├── charts/                   # gitignored PNGs (Phase 1)
    ├── pathway_graph.graphml
    ├── phase1_summary.md
    └── phase2/                   # gitignored Phase 2 outputs
        ├── tables/
        ├── charts/
        ├── graphs/
        ├── reports/
        ├── family_discovery_report.md
        └── clinical_review_pack.md
```

Two files share the `09_` prefix: `09_data_quality.sql` is Phase 1's and
`09_family_features.sql` is Phase 2's. The prefix orders files for readability only —
queries are addressed by name, and the loader rejects duplicate names across files.

The Phase 2 module list is three modules longer than the seven the brief named:
`family_config.py`, `family_charts.py` and `family_report.py` were split out so that
configuration loading, figure composition and document generation do not sit inside the
runner.

Queries are addressed by name, not filename. Each `.sql` file contains one or more
blocks introduced by a `-- name: <identifier>` marker, and `db.QueryLibrary` loads them
all, rejecting duplicate names across files and any statement that is not read-only.

`sql/00_reference_data.sql` and `sql/09_data_quality.sql` are additions to the
originally specified eight files: the first holds the dimension extracts that the other
analyses join against, the second holds the integrity checks behind
`outputs/tables/data_quality.csv`.

